WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.550
Crown corporation oversight.

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.876
Billions without accountability.

00:00:04.876 --> 00:00:11.965
Canada operates approximately forty-seven Crown corporations controlling over four hundred billion dollars in combined assets.

00:00:11.965 --> 00:00:20.079
These entities operate at arm's length from Parliament — meaning they deploy massive public resources with less direct oversight than government departments.

00:00:20.079 --> 00:00:25.431
The Canada Mortgage and Housing Corporation insures over five hundred billion in mortgages.

00:00:25.431 --> 00:00:29.693
The Canada Infrastructure Bank has a thirty-five billion dollar mandate.

00:00:29.693 --> 00:00:33.522
Export Development Canada provides billions in financing.

00:00:33.522 --> 00:00:38.545
The CBC receives one point four billion in annual parliamentary appropriation.

00:00:38.545 --> 00:00:42.818
Each operates with its own board of directors, appointed by the government.

00:00:42.818 --> 00:00:46.693
The Auditor General has limited special examination authority.

00:00:46.693 --> 00:00:50.977
Parliamentary committees can study them but cannot direct their operations.

00:00:50.977 --> 00:00:53.318
Board appointments are political.

00:00:53.318 --> 00:00:56.022
The accountability gap is structural.

00:00:56.022 --> 00:00:58.056
The oversight table.

00:00:58.056 --> 00:01:02.886
Crown corporations compared to government departments on key accountability measures.

00:01:02.886 --> 00:01:14.136
Government departments: full AG audit access, Treasury Board expenditure oversight, ministerial responsibility, parliamentary estimates debate, ATIP coverage, and public service staffing rules.

00:01:14.136 --> 00:01:27.420
Crown corporations: AG special examination only every ten years, limited Treasury Board oversight, arm's-length from ministerial direction, limited estimates debate, partial ATIP coverage, and exemption from many public service staffing rules.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.352
The accountability deficit is structural.

00:01:30.352 --> 00:01:32.386
The comparison table.

00:01:32.386 --> 00:01:42.499
Government departments have full AG audit access, Treasury Board oversight, ministerial accountability, parliamentary estimates, ATIP coverage, and public service staffing rules.

00:01:42.499 --> 00:01:54.318
Crown corporations have only special examination every ten years, limited Treasury Board oversight, arm's length ministerial relationship, limited estimates debate, partial ATIP coverage, and their own staffing authority.

00:01:54.318 --> 00:01:57.761
Key Crown corporations and their accountability gaps.

00:01:57.761 --> 00:01:59.659
Four case studies.

00:01:59.659 --> 00:02:02.420
The structural accountability problems.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:04.693
Three fundamental issues.

00:02:04.693 --> 00:02:07.136
The accountability architecture.

00:02:07.136 --> 00:02:12.238
Crown corporations represent a documented gap in Canadian democratic accountability.

00:02:12.238 --> 00:02:16.159
They control more assets than most government departments combined.

00:02:16.159 --> 00:02:18.727
Their boards are politically appointed.

00:02:18.727 --> 00:02:22.647
Their AG scrutiny is periodic rather than continuous.

00:02:22.647 --> 00:02:26.636
Their arm's-length status shields them from parliamentary accountability.

00:02:26.636 --> 00:02:35.840
This is the same pattern documented across TENET5 investigations: accountability mechanisms that appear robust on paper but produce zero enforcement in practice.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:41.238
The accountability scorecard grades ten oversight bodies and finds zero enforcement.

00:02:41.238 --> 00:02:51.602
Crown corporations add another layer to that architecture: entities that deploy hundreds of billions in public resources with structural insulation from the accountability mechanisms that already don't enforce.
